# Part 1: Getting Started

sibl is a static and dynamic linker written in Zig 0.17.0. It has no external dependencies. It reads ELF, PE/COFF, Mach-O, and static archives. After that, it resolves symbols, groups sections, builds segments, and applies relocations.

># [Important]($block.attrs('warning'))
>sibl does not write a runnable binary yet. Phases 1 to 9 are ready, and binary emission is Phase 13. For now, the `sibl` command is an inspection pass. It shows what the linker found and how it plans the output.

## What you need

- Zig 0.17.0
- Git, if you clone the source

The project uses only the Zig standard library, so you do not need to install extra packages.

## Build from source

First, clone the repository and move into it:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/zig-foundry/sibl.git
cd sibl
```

Then build the executable:

```sh
zig build
```

The binary lands in `zig-out/bin/sibl`. You can also run it through the build system:

```sh
zig build run -- --help
```

Before you change code, run the full test suite:

```sh
zig build test
```

## Your first run

Make a small object file, then pass it to sibl. Because sibl does not write output yet, this step only prints the inspection report.

```sh
mkdir sibl-demo
cd sibl-demo

cat > hello.zig <<'EOF'
export fn _start() void {}
EOF

zig build-obj hello.zig -femit-bin=hello.o
/path/to/sibl/zig-out/bin/sibl hello.o
```

Replace `/path/to/sibl` with the directory where you built sibl. The report starts like this:

```
sibl configuration:
  Target Arch   : x86_64 (64-bit, little)
  Binary Format : elf
  Layout Template: linux-elf (base=0x400000, page=4096)
  Page Size     : 4096 bytes
  Output File   : a.out
  Entry Point   : _start
  Shared Mode   : false
```

The report ends with the resolved entry point:

```
  Entry Point '_start':
    Address       : 0x401000
    Section       : .text
    Executable    : true
```

If your object exports `main` instead of `_start`, pass the entry name with `-e`:

```sh
zig build-obj hello.zig -femit-bin=hello.o
/path/to/sibl/zig-out/bin/sibl -e main hello.o
```

Without `-e main`, sibl tries `_start` and exits with `EntrySymbolNotFound`. This is normal because the default entry name for Linux ELF is `_start`.

## Read the report

The report has several blocks. Each block shows one stage of the link plan.

| Block | What it shows |
| --- | --- |
| Configuration | Target architecture, binary format, layout template, output name, and entry name. |
| Input files | Parsed headers, sections, symbols, and relocations for each input. |
| Resolved symbols | Defined and undefined symbols, binding, visibility, and value. |
| Boundary symbols | Start and end markers such as `etext`, `edata`, and `end`. |
| Section layout | Merged output sections, virtual addresses, file offsets, sizes, and flags. |
| Segments | Loadable groups with `filesz`, `memsz`, and permission flags. |
| Entry point | Final virtual address of the entry symbol. |

Symbols marked with `*` are exportable. The program prints up to 32 symbols, then a count of the rest.

## Command-line flags

| Flag | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `-o <file>`, `--output <file>` | Set the output file name. The default is `a.out`, or `a.exe` for PE. |
| `-e <symbol>`, `--entry <symbol>`, `--entry=<symbol>` | Set the entry symbol. The default is `_start`. |
| `-m <emulation>` | Set the emulation, for example `elf_x86_64`, `elf_aarch64`, `elf64lriscv`, or `i386pep`. |
| `-L <dir>`, `-L<dir>` | Add a library search directory. The parser stores it for later phases. |
| `-l <name>`, `-l<name>` | Add a library name. The parser stores it for later phases. |
| `-shared`, `--shared` | Build a shared library. This also relaxes the undefined-symbol and entry checks. |
| `-static`, `--static` | Request a static link. The flag is reserved for later phases. |
| `-s`, `--strip-all` | Remove all symbols from the output. The flag is reserved for the emission phase. |
| `-S`, `--strip-debug` | Remove debug sections from the output. The flag is reserved for the emission phase. |
| `--format <fmt>` | Set the output format: `auto`, `elf`, `coff`, or `macho`. |
| `--region N=A:Z` | Define a memory region with name, address, and size. |
| `--place P=D` | Move matching sections into an output section or a region. |
| `--pin S=A` | Pin an output section at an exact virtual address. |
| `--defsym SYM=EXPR` | Define an absolute symbol. |
| `--` | Stop option parsing. Every later argument is an input file. |
| `-h`, `--help` | Print the help text. |

Some flags do not change files on disk yet. sibl accepts them now, but the writer stages that use them are still in the roadmap.

## Placement rules

Placement rules help with embedded maps or custom memory layouts. You can define regions, move sections into them, and pin a section at an exact address:

```sh
/path/to/sibl/zig-out/bin/sibl \
  --region FLASH=0x08000000:0x10000 \
  --region RAM=0x20000000:0x8000 \
  --place '.isr*=FLASH' \
  --pin '.vector=0x08000040' \
  crt.o main.o driver.o
```

- `--place PATTERN=DEST` moves matching sections into an output section. If `DEST` names a region, the sections are allocated inside that region.
- `--pin PATTERN=ADDR` sets the virtual address of the holding output section.
- `--region NAME=ADDR:SIZE` defines a region. Sections fill it in order, with alignment, until the region is full.

The first matching rule wins. Put catch-all `*` rules last.

## Define symbols

`--defsym` adds absolute symbols after sibl collects input symbols:

```sh
/path/to/sibl/zig-out/bin/sibl --defsym VERSION=0x100 --defsym BUILD=4096 main.o

/path/to/sibl/zig-out/bin/sibl --defsym ENTRY=main+0x10 --defsym LIMIT=ENTRY-4 main.o
```

An expression can be a hex or decimal constant, an existing symbol, or a `+` / `-` combination. Do not put spaces in the expression. Later specs can use symbols from earlier specs. A second strong definition of the same name stops the link with an error.

## Default layout templates

When you do not pin a custom base, sibl picks a built-in template:

| Target | Base address | Default entry | Output |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Linux ELF | `0x400000` | `_start` | `a.out` |
| Windows PE 64 | `0x140000000` | `mainCRTStartup` | `a.exe` |
| Windows PE 32 | `0x400000` | `mainCRTStartup` | `a.exe` |
| macOS Mach-O | `0x100000000` | `_main` | `a.out` |
| Bare-metal | `0` | `_start` | `a.out` |

sibl selects the bare-metal template when you pass any `--region` rule. After address assignment, the template adds these boundary symbols if the names are still free:

- `__executable_start`
- `etext` and `_etext`
- `edata` and `_edata`
- `__bss_start`
- `end` and `_end`

## Use sibl as a library

Import the root module and use the exported types. The example below shows the main pieces:

```zig
const sibl = @import("sibl");

var table = sibl.SymbolTable.init(allocator);
defer table.deinit();

var layout = sibl.Layout.init(allocator, 0x400000, 4096);
defer layout.deinit();

layout.computeAddresses();
const entry_point = try sibl.resolveEntry(&table, &layout, "_start");
```

Add symbols and input sections before `computeAddresses()`. Public types are re-exported from `src/root.zig`.

You can also generate HTML API docs:

```sh
zig build-lib src/root.zig -femit-docs
```

## Project layout

```
src/
├── alloc.zig          Phase 1.2  — arena and scratch allocators
├── args.zig           Phase 1.3  — CLI argument parser
├── target.zig         Phase 1.4  — target detection and page size
├── file.zig           Phase 1.5  — memory-mapped file reader
├── elf.zig            Phase 2    — ELF parsing
├── strtab.zig         Phase 2.4  — string table reader and builder
├── coff.zig           Phase 3    — PE/COFF parsing
├── macho.zig          Phase 4    — Mach-O parsing
├── archive.zig        Phase 5    — static archive (.a / .lib) parsing
├── symbol.zig         Phase 6    — global symbol table and resolution
├── layout.zig         Phase 7    — section grouping, segments, size accounting
├── reloc.zig          Phase 8    — relocation engine (x86_64, AArch64)
├── entry.zig          Phase 9.1  — entry point resolution
├── section_rules.zig  Phase 9.2  — placement rules and memory regions
├── defsym.zig         Phase 9.3  — --defsym symbol assignment
├── templates.zig      Phase 9.4  — default layout templates
├── root.zig           Public re-exports
└── main.zig           Linker executable entry point
```

## Environment variables

sibl does not read environment variables. All settings come from command-line flags and input files.

## Exit codes

sibl uses two exit codes.

- `0`: the inspection pass finished.
- `1`: an error stopped the run, such as a missing input, a parse error, an unresolved symbol, or an invalid flag.

## Common problems

### `no input files provided`

Pass at least one input file after the flags. sibl accepts object files, archives, and already linked binaries.

### `cannot resolve entry point '_start'`

Your input has no `_start` symbol. Use `-e <name>` to choose a different entry, or export `_start` from your source.

### `unresolved symbols`

sibl checks that every symbol is defined before it stops. Fix the missing definition, link the object that provides it, or pass `-shared` when you build a shared library.

### No output file appears

This is expected. The binary writer is Phase 13. The current command only prints the inspection report and the planned layout.

### `invalid target format`

Use one of `auto`, `elf`, `coff`, or `macho` after `--format`.

## Next step

Part 2 explains the core concepts behind the stages you just saw. It covers object formats, symbols, layout, relocations, and templates in more detail.
